No.  I'd be trying to make it a better game.  The reason why is 
about a lot of things, not least of which is my idea of what 
makes a game better.  

Games that are literally impossible to win are no fun, and with 
the exception of sex, games that are very easy to win are no fun 
either. 

Games are made better by having the player need to make choices 
with serious in-game consequences either way.  They are made 
better by having a lot of different viable strategies, allowing 
wins that are very different. 

The key thing to balance, IMO, is the line between not enough 
choices (so the player never gets a chance to develop a strategy 
at all) and too many (so the player can pick the SAME favorite 
strategy again and again and again), because neither of those 
situations is fun.

So I'd give players the option to choose among strategies; for 
example, am I an evil/chaotic character who uses poison, curses, 
and unholy water routinely, and barters for power with a malicious 
deity whose sense of humor places me at risk? Am I a good/lawful 
character who uses holy water, blessings, and enjoys the favor 
of a benevolent god who's not nearly as easy to bribe but also 
won't gratuitously screw with me?  Or do I pretty much ignore 
such existential questions, avoid currying or calling on divine 
favor excessively, and be wary of things that could make my 
god angry?  I'd try to make each strategy equally capable of 
winning, but preserve this as an axis that requires a conscious 
choice of playstyle and gear which differentiates characters.  

On a less grand and arbitrary scale, consider the choices you 
already make about which weapons to train.  When advancing 
something to expert means leaving something else at basic, 
you're making a strategy decision that differentiates your 
character, at least a bit, from other characters. 

The reason I'm down on wishes and magic markers is because they
give players too many choices.  The consequence of this is that 
they promote uniformity in characters and also that their use by 
spoiled players is almost completely uniform.  They effectively 
eliminate all the viable choices except for a few considered "best," 
by making it too easy for a player to get exactly what s/he wants 
instead of having to think about how to evolve a viable strategy 
with what s/he gets.  I'd be pretty busy trying to make a lot of 
different choices as equally-viable as possible, which involves 
nerfing some things and buffing others, but seeing the 
possibilities of an unfamiliar strategy (instead of picking your 
favorite again) should be an essential skill in winning, and 
wide-open "pick anything" situations like wishes and magic markers 
don't exercise that skill. These items give too many choices 
instead of not enough.

My objection to farming puddings for items, aside from the obvious 
in-game logic breakage, is the same: gobs of stuff will include 
whatever stuff the player wants, thus giving too many choices and 
not forcing the player to make and live with an unfamiliar decision.  
If you follow this line of thinking, you can probably see where it 
leads with respect to things like polypiling too. 


